INT. HOSPITAL BARGE "MERCY ELEANOR" - WARD DECK - NIGHT

A converted river freighter. Twelve cots on a steel deck, curtains on ceiling rails, an IV pole tied to a pillar with bungee cord. Through round portholes, the lights of a riverbank town drift by.

DR. ISMAIL KARIMI, 50s, rumpled scrubs, reading glasses hanging from one ear, signs a chart at a card table nailed to the floor. NURSE BRONWEN ACKERLEY, 40s, tall, silver braid, checks an IV bag with a flashlight. ORDERLY TOBIAS FINCH, 20s, headphones around his neck, mops in lazy circles. DR. LINH THAI, 30s, resident, sits cross-legged on a cot, stethoscope draped, eating crackers from a sleeve. And at the end of the row, MRS. ODETTA WRAY, 70s, the only patient awake, sits up in a cot with a blanket over her knees.

ISMAIL
Bed six, no change.

BRONWEN
Bed six, no change.

ISMAIL
Bed nine?

LINH
Temp is ninety-nine point one.

ISMAIL
Fine. Leave it.

LINH
I was going to.

TOBIAS
Do we have more mop heads?

BRONWEN
Closet, port side.

TOBIAS
Port is?

BRONWEN
Left.

TOBIAS
Thanks.

The barge shifts. A low groan runs through the hull. Nobody reacts. The IV pole sways against its bungee.

MRS. WRAY
Doctor.

ISMAIL
Yes, Mrs. Wray.

MRS. WRAY
Is it always so slow at night?

ISMAIL
Usually it's slower.

MRS. WRAY
Then I'm in luck.

ISMAIL
Are you in pain?

MRS. WRAY
Only boredom.

LINH
(through crackers)
I can read you something.

MRS. WRAY
What have you got?

LINH
The back of the cracker box.

MRS. WRAY
Go ahead.

LINH
"Made with whole grains. Best before March."

MRS. WRAY
Beautiful.

Bronwen stifles a laugh. Ismail glances over his glasses, almost smiles.

TOBIAS
Dr. Karimi, should I do the starboard side next?

ISMAIL
If you like.

TOBIAS
I do like.

ISMAIL
Then carry on.

The barge's horn sounds, low and soft, a note across water.

BRONWEN
That's Calder Landing.

ISMAIL
We dock in an hour.

LINH
Do we get shore leave?

ISMAIL
We get paperwork.

LINH
Same thing.

ISMAIL
Dr. Thai.

LINH
Sorry.

Mrs. Wray settles into her pillow. Her eyes close. A pulse monitor beeps in a slow, steady tick.

Bronwen adjusts the blanket across her knees. Tucks it neat.

BRONWEN
(softly)
Sleep well.

MRS. WRAY
(eyes closed)
Everything's fine?

BRONWEN
Everything's fine.

She turns off the flashlight. The ward falls into amber, quiet, familiar dark.
